\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{A norm-one even unit can leave the vector space in dimension six}

\p{Let #{K} be a field of characteristic different from two, and let
#{(V,Q)} be a finite-dimensional nondegenerate quadratic space containing
six orthogonal vectors #{e_1,\ldots,e_6} with #{Q(e_i)\ne0}. Put

##{\omega=e_1\cdots e_6,\qquad
\delta=\prod_{i=1}^6 Q(e_i).}

Now #{\omega} is even, #{\operatorname{rev}(\omega)=-\omega}, and
#{\omega^2=-\delta1}. It anticommutes with each of the six displayed
vectors. If #{a,b\in K} satisfy #{a^2+\delta b^2=1}, then

##{x=a+b\omega,\qquad x^{-1}=\operatorname{rev}(x)=a-b\omega}

is an even unit of reverse norm one. Its action on a displayed basis vector is

##{xe_i x^{-1}=(a^2-\delta b^2)e_i+2ab\omega e_i.}}

\p{For #{ab\ne0}, the last term is a nonzero degree-five monomial,
up to a scalar. Extend the six vectors to an orthogonal basis; independence
of the Clifford monomials shows that the displayed conjugate is not a
vector. Since #{x} is even, ordinary and twisted conjugation agree.
Thus #{x} is not Lipschitz and cannot belong to Spin, despite satisfying
the norm equation
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.notMem_lipschitzGroup_of_mem_evenUnitaryGroup_of_coe_eq}.
Only the selected six vectors are used: in higher dimension #{\omega}
commutes with an additional orthogonal vector, which does not affect this
counterexample.}

\p{For a concrete compact example, take #{K=\mathbb R} and
#{Q(v)=v_1^2+\cdots+v_6^2}. With #{a=b=1/\sqrt2}, one obtains

##{x=\frac{1+\omega}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\operatorname{rev}(x)x=1,\qquad
xe_1x^{-1}=\omega e_1=-e_2e_3e_4e_5e_6.}

Porteous \citet{Chapter 16, p. 146}{porteous1995clifford}
exhibits the unnormalized element #{1+\omega} of norm two.}

\p{Gallier \citet{Section 1.4, closing Remark, p. 31}{gallier2014clifford}
uses the normalized element above.}

\p{Their compact generators square to #{-1}. With that choice the same
six-vector product still squares to #{-1}, but
#{\omega e_1=+e_2e_3e_4e_5e_6}. Both signs give the same obstruction.
The calculation here uses the positive-square convention of
\ref{fcap-002H}.}

\p{Over any infinite #{K} as above, the conic always supplies
#{a,b\ne0}. Indeed, choose #{t\ne0} with #{1+\delta t^2\ne0} and
#{1-\delta t^2\ne0}, and set

##{a=\frac{1-\delta t^2}{1+\delta t^2},\qquad
b=\frac{2t}{1+\delta t^2}.}

Only finitely many values of #{t} are excluded, and substitution gives
#{a^2+\delta b^2=1}. Consequently the Spin image is a proper subgroup
of the even-unitary group for every nondegenerate form of dimension at
least six over an infinite field of characteristic different from two
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.range_spinGroup_toUnits_ne_evenUnitaryGroup_of_six_le_finrank}.
This field extension follows from the displayed calculation; the cited
book passages concern real spaces. Over a finite field the conditional
six-vector construction remains valid, but this infinitude argument makes
no assertion that a suitable conic point exists.}
